public with sharing class LeadContactMatcher {

    /**
     * For each lead, returns the Id of the first contact (in `contacts` order)
     * whose email matches the lead's email. Matching ignores case and
     * leading/trailing whitespace. A lead or contact with a blank email never
     * matches. Performs no SOQL and no DML.
     *
     * Implemented as an O(n + m) hash-map join rather than a nested loop:
     * at the stated bounds (3,000 leads x 3,000 contacts = up to 9,000,000
     * comparisons) the nested loop can exceed the Apex CPU time limit inside
     * the import transaction (observed: System.LimitException: Apex CPU time
     * limit exceeded). This version normalizes each email once and does a
     * constant-time lookup per lead, and produces identical results to the
     * nested-loop formulation: for duplicates, the first contact in
     * `contacts` order wins.
     */
    public static List<Id> matchByEmail(List<Lead> leads, List<Contact> contacts) {
        List<Id> result = new List<Id>();

        if (leads == null || leads.isEmpty()) {
            return result;
        }

        // Build the lookup index once: normalized email -> Id of the FIRST
        // contact in `contacts` order with that email. Later duplicates are
        // ignored, which preserves the "first one in contacts order" rule.
        Map<String, Id> firstContactIdByEmail = new Map<String, Id>();
        if (contacts != null) {
            for (Contact c : contacts) {
                String key = normalize(c.Email);
                if (String.isNotBlank(key) && !firstContactIdByEmail.containsKey(key)) {
                    firstContactIdByEmail.put(key, c.Id);
                }
            }
        }

        // One O(1) lookup per lead; blank lead emails never match.
        for (Lead l : leads) {
            String key = normalize(l.Email);
            Id matchedContactId = null;
            if (String.isNotBlank(key)) {
                matchedContactId = firstContactIdByEmail.get(key);
            }
            result.add(matchedContactId);
        }

        return result;
    }

    // Lowercase + trim; null-safe. After trimming, an all-whitespace email
    // becomes the empty string, which String.isNotBlank treats as blank, so
    // blank emails can never match.
    private static String normalize(String email) {
        if (email == null) {
            return null;
        }
        String trimmed = email.trim();
        return trimmed.toLowerCase();
    }
}
```

---

**What changed and why**

- **Root cause of the failure:** the nested loop really does hit the Apex CPU time limit at the stated maximums. 3,000 leads × 3,000 contacts is up to ~9 million `String.equals` calls, each on freshly trimmed/lowercased strings; executed inside the import transaction that pushed the test (`FB_LeadContactMatcherTest.importOf3000`) past the 10-second CPU limit. CPU time is a hard, transaction-wide governor, so "it runs inside the import transaction" is exactly what makes the quadratic version unsafe, not fine.
- **Fix:** replaced the nested loop with a one-pass hash join. Contact emails are normalized once into a `Map<String, Id>` (first contact wins per email, preserving the required tie-break rule), then each lead is a single O(1) lookup. Total work is ~6,000 normalizations plus 3,000 lookups instead of up to 9M comparisons — same output, orders of magnitude less CPU.
- **Semantics preserved exactly:** same size and order as `leads`; case- and trim-insensitive matching; blank lead or contact emails never match (`isNotBlank` guard on both sides, with all-whitespace trimming to empty); `null` when no contact matches; no SOQL, no DML; null/empty input lists handled safely.
- I removed the nested-loop method entirely rather than keep it as a trap for a future caller — if you ever want it back for tiny in-memory comparisons, keep it behind a size guard, but for import-scale matching the indexed version is the correct implementation.

